<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <!--
      ContinuousIntegrationBuild forces deterministic source paths into the PDB
      (used by SourceLink). Only set it under CI: doing it locally too replaces
      real file paths with unresolvable ones in a debugger.
    -->
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true' or '$(TF_BUILD)' == 'true' or '$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      CS1591 (missing XML doc comment) is off repo-wide, not just under
      SwissEphNet/CPort/: this whole library's public API (55+ KB of it, most
      of it a mechanical C port) has no XML docs today, and that is a known,
      accepted state, not something to warn about on every build. A per-directory
      .editorconfig severity override is not used for this one, because .editorconfig
      severity does not reliably suppress every compiler-level (CS-prefixed)
      diagnostic the same way it does analyzer (CA/IDE) diagnostics, and NoWarn is
      the long-standing, reliable way to turn this specific warning off. This must
      stay a project-wide NoWarn, not a style choice a nested .editorconfig can
      quietly re-enable.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    <!--
      Explicitly OFF, not merely defaulted off. SwissEphNet/CPort is a 1:1
      transliteration of the Swiss Ephemeris C source (see CONTRIBUTING.md);
      turning on nullable reference types would produce tens of thousands of
      warnings across 1.5 MB of ported C with no bug-finding value, and the
      next person to "helpfully" flip it on needs to see it was a deliberate
      choice, not an oversight.
    -->
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <!-- Packing happens explicitly (dotnet pack), not as a side effect of building. -->
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>false</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately NOT setting IsTrimmable/IsAotCompatible here. An earlier version of
    this file did (conditioned on TargetFramework in Directory.Build.targets, since a
    single-TargetFramework project has not yet assigned $(TargetFramework) at the
    point Directory.Build.props itself is imported), but that has two problems:

    1. The condition matched net10.0-windows too (SweWin, a WinForms app, which is
       not trim/AOT-safe), and there was no way for a project to opt back out:
       Directory.Build.targets is imported after a project's own PropertyGroup, so an
       explicit <IsTrimmable>false</IsTrimmable> in SweWin.csproj would have been
       silently overwritten.

    2. IsTrimmable=true is stamped into the shipped assembly as
       AssemblyMetadata("IsTrimmable","True"), a public promise to the trimmer that
       nothing in this repo validates. Attempting to validate it here (publishing a
       small net10.0 console app that references SwissEphNet with PublishTrimmed=true
       or PublishAot=true) fails immediately with NETSDK1124/NETSDK1207: MSBuild's
       cross-project target-framework negotiation evaluates SwissEphNet's
       netstandard2.0 leg too, even though the consuming app only ever resolves the
       net10.0 asset, and netstandard2.0 supports neither trimming nor AOT. Making
       that validation actually work is real, separate work (either restructuring how
       multi-targeting interacts with publish, or scoping the check some other way),
       not something to bolt on to a toolchain-retarget PR.

    Shipping the promise without validating it is worse than not making the promise
    yet. Revisit once there is an actual trim/AOT smoke check.
  -->

</Project>
